  "name": "Question 5",
  "problem": "A particle $P$ is traveling along the $x$-axis with displacement $s(t)$ from a fixed origin $O$ given by\n\n$$\ns(t)=t\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(t)-{t}^{2},\\,t>0.\n$$\n\nFind the time at which the acceleration is zero, and find the value of the velocity at this moment.\n\nThe acceleration is zero at $t=$\n{{field-1}}, and the velocity at this moment is\n$v=$\n{{field-2}}.",
  "worked_solution": "First, we calculate $v(t)$ by differentiating $s(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(t)-{t}^{2}) \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}\u2061(t)+t\\cdot \\frac{1}{t}-2t \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(t)+1-2t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate $v(t)$ to find $a(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(t)+1-2t) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{t}-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we put $a(t)=0$ to find the time when the acceleration is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{t}-2 & =0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{t} & =2 \\\\ t & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the acceleration is zero when $t=\\frac{1}{2}.$ To find the velocity at this moment, we substitute this back into $v(t)$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v\\,(\\frac{1}{2}) & =ln\\,(\\frac{1}{2})+1-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =ln\\,(\\frac{1}{2})+1-1 \\\\  & =ln\\,(\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the acceleration is zero at $t=\\frac{1}{2},$ and the velocity at this moment is $v=-\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(2).$",
